talk(134,'1967-09-25','D. B. Fuchs','P000212-P000217','Characteristic operations and universal frame spaces',r'The continuation headed Fuchs starts on P000213 and runs through P000215. It computes the new characteristic cohomology from invariant forms, identifies natural operations taking principal bundles to other principal bundles, and constructs universal objects from frames in arbitrary products. These are precise counterparts of \citet[\S\S1, 2, 4]{gelfandfuchs1967topology}; the ordinary constant sheaf must be distinguished from the sheaf of continuous real functions.')
item(134,'P000213','definition','The bundle category and its characteristic operations',r'The free action, the global fiberwise linear-chart condition, and equivariant bundle morphisms are restated. \(h_{\mathrm{alg}}(G)\) denotes all characteristic classes formed from a contravariant functor \(h\).',r'Fix a closed subgroup \(G\subset GL(n,\mathbb R)\). Objects are the principal bundles over Hausdorff bases satisfying the global chart condition of 1967-132.5; morphisms are equivariant maps covering continuous base maps. A characteristic operation assigns \(c(\xi)\in h(X)\) to each object naturally under pullback. No vanishing of the chart map outside a prescribed neighborhood is required.',r'\citet[\S1 and the beginning of \S2]{gelfandfuchs1967topology} formalizes this category and its natural operations.',('gelfandfuchs1967topology',),cls='A',literal='correct')
item(134,'P000213','definition','Cohomology with the sheaf of continuous real functions',r'The coefficient sheaf called \(\mathbb R\) consists of germs of continuous real functions. The notes say \(H^q(X,\mathbb R)=0\) for paracompact \(X\).',r'Write \(\mathcal C_X\) for the sheaf of continuous real functions. On a paracompact Hausdorff space it is fine, so \(H^q(X;\mathcal C_X)=0\) for \(q>0\). Degree zero is \(C(X,\mathbb R)\), usually nonzero. The characteristic groups \(H^q_{\mathrm{alg}}(G;\mathbb R)\) classify natural classes with values in this sheaf cohomology over the enlarged category, not ordinary singular-cohomology characteristic classes.',r'The notation and vanishing are the setup of \citet[\S2]{gelfandfuchs1967topology}. Positive degree is implicit in the seminar parenthesis and is restored here.',('gelfandfuchs1967topology',),cls='A',reconstruction=r'The notation \(\mathcal C_X\) separates the variable-function sheaf from the constant real sheaf used in the compact-dual calculation.')
item(134,'P000213-P000214','theorem','Compact-dual calculation in the semisimple case',r'For semisimple \(G\) with finitely many components, \(H^q_{\mathrm{alg}}(G)=H^q(\bar G/K;\mathbb R)\), with constant coefficients on the right; \(K\) is maximal compact and \(\bar G\) a compact form.',r'For connected semisimple linear \(G\), let \(\mathfrak g=\mathfrak k\oplus\mathfrak p\) be its Cartan decomposition, and choose the compact dual pair with Lie algebras \(\mathfrak g_u=\mathfrak k\oplus i\mathfrak p\) and \(\mathfrak k\). Then \(H^*_{\mathrm{alg}}(G;\mathbb R)\cong H^*(G_u/K_u;\mathbb R)\), with the compatible global compact dual and component actions included when \(G\) is disconnected.',r'This is \citet[Theorem 2.9]{gelfandfuchs1967topology}. It concerns real cohomology and the compact symmetric dual, not replacement of the original ordinary classifying space \(BG\) by \(G_u/K_u\).',('gelfandfuchs1967topology',),cls='A',reconstruction=r'The Lie-algebra construction avoids an unjustified assertion that every chosen maximal compact subgroup embeds unchanged in an arbitrarily chosen compact global form.')
item(134,'P000214','claim','The linear-group example and the sphere ambiguity',r'For \(GL(n,\mathbb R)\), the notes name \(U(n)\) and \(O(n)\), then write: second order, as for \(S^2\).',r'The reductive compact dual for \(GL(n,\mathbb R)/O(n)\) is \(U(n)/O(n)\). The rank-two semisimple example with spherical dual is instead \(SL(2,\mathbb R)/SO(2)\), whose compact dual is \(SU(2)/SO(2)\simeq S^2\). These spaces cannot be conflated: \(\dim U(2)/O(2)=3\), whereas \(\dim S^2=2\).',r'\citet[Theorem 2.9 and its examples]{gelfandfuchs1967topology} supports the semisimple example. The scan does not insert \(SL(2)\) before the sphere remark, so the intended change of group is an emendation, not a resolved transcription.',('gelfandfuchs1967topology',),cls='A',literal='not determinable',match='partial',reconstruction=r'The missing transition from the reductive \(GL(n)\) example to \(SL(2)\) is flagged, not silently supplied as notebook wording.',confidence='medium')
item(134,'P000214','theorem','Invariant forms compute the characteristic cohomology',r'For a nonsemisimple group one takes \(G/K\), its \(G\)-invariant differential forms, and the cohomology of their differential complex.',r'For a closed linear Lie group in the category under discussion, \[H^q_{\mathrm{alg}}(G;\mathbb R)\cong H^q\bigl(\Omega^*(G/K)^G,d\bigr).\] Exterior differentiation preserves invariance. This is invariant-form, or relative Lie-algebra, cohomology; it need not equal the de Rham cohomology of the contractible space \(G/K\).',r'The exact result is \citet[Theorem 2.1]{gelfandfuchs1967topology}. The qualification about invariant forms explains how a nonzero answer survives when the underlying homogeneous space is contractible.',('gelfandfuchs1967topology',),cls='A',literal='correct')
item(134,'P000214','theorem','Compact groups and the additive real group',r'For compact groups everything is trivial. For \(\mathbb R\), the answer is \(\mathbb R\) in degrees 0 and 1 and zero above, with representatives \(1\) and \(dx\).',r'If \(G\) is compact, the invariant-form complex on \(G/G\) has \(H^0=\mathbb R\) and \(H^q=0\) for \(q>0\). For \(G=(\mathbb R,+)\), the complex of translation-invariant forms is \(\mathbb R\to\mathbb R\,dx\to0\) with zero differential, giving exactly the stated groups.',r'These are direct instances of \citet[Theorem 2.1]{gelfandfuchs1967topology}. The word trivial refers to positive degrees, not degree zero.',('gelfandfuchs1967topology',),cls='A',reconstruction=r'The degree-zero exception and the zero differential are supplied explicitly.')
item(134,'P000214','theorem','Natural principal H-bundles are induced by homomorphisms',r'\(EH_{\mathrm{alg}}(G)=\operatorname{Hom}(G,H)\), more precisely continuous homomorphisms modulo conjugacy in \(H\).',r'For the specified category of principal \(G\)-bundles and a topological group \(H\), natural assignments of isomorphism classes of ordinary locally trivial principal \(H\)-bundles correspond to \(\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathrm{cont}}(G,H)/H\). A homomorphism \(\phi\) acts by extension of structure group \(E\mapsto E\times_\phi H\); conjugate homomorphisms give naturally isomorphic assignments.',r'This is \citet[Theorem 4.1]{gelfandfuchs1967topology}. The paper allows ordinary local triviality for \(H\)-bundles; when \(H\) is a Lie group, using the enlarged condition gives the same answer.',('gelfandfuchs1967topology',),cls='A',literal='correct')
item(134,'P000215','construction','Frames in a product and universal bundle morphisms',r'\(\mathcal E_G^I\) consists of \(n\)-frames in \(\mathbb R^I\) with the Tychonoff topology. \(GL(n,\mathbb R)\) and \(G\) act, and \(S_G^I=\mathcal E_G^I/G\); every bundle maps into one for sufficiently large \(I\).',r'Let \(F_n(\mathbb R^I)\) be ordered linearly independent \(n\)-tuples, equivalently injective linear maps \(\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R^I\), with the subspace topology inherited from \((\mathbb R^I)^n\). Precomposition gives a free action of \(G\subset GL(n,\mathbb R)\). Taking sufficiently many global charts gives a bundle morphism into \(F_n(\mathbb R^I)\to F_n(\mathbb R^I)/G\). The total space depends on \(n,I\), while the quotient and action depend on \(G\).',r'\citet[\S1, Proposition 1.1]{gelfandfuchs1967topology} proves this exact universal construction.',('gelfandfuchs1967topology',),cls='A',literal='correct',problem='GP-1967-01')
item(134,'P000215','theorem','A countable-coordinate lemma for frame spaces',r'For every continuous real function on \(\mathcal E_G^I\), there is \(J\subset I\) with \(I\setminus J\) at most countable such that its restriction to \(\mathcal E_G^J\) is constant.',r'Embed \(F_n(\mathbb R^J)\) into \(F_n(\mathbb R^I)\) by setting coordinates outside \(J\) equal to zero. Every continuous real-valued function on the latter has a constant restriction to such a subspace for some co-countable \(J\). The statement does not guarantee a nonempty subspace when \(I\) is itself countable and too many coordinates have been removed.',r'The exact lemma is \citet[Proposition 1.3]{gelfandfuchs1967topology}; it is one of the technical reasons large product-indexed universal spaces behave differently from familiar CW models.',('gelfandfuchs1967topology',),cls='A',literal='correct',reconstruction=r'The zero-extension inclusion and the possibly vacuous small-index case are stated explicitly.')
correction('P000213','Paracompact sheaf cohomology',r'H^q(X,\mathbb R)=0 for paracompact X',r'H^q(X,\mathcal C_X)=0 for q>0','The notebook uses the sheaf of continuous functions; degree zero is C(X,R), not zero.')
correction('P000214','Second-order compact dual','GL(2) example followed by as for S^2','S^2 is the compact dual for SL(2,R)/SO(2); the intended group switch is unstated','The scan reads second order. U(2)/O(2) is three-dimensional; the published semisimple example is SL(2).','both','ambiguity')

talk(135,'1967-10-02','D. B. Fuchs','P000215-P000219','Elementary G-objects and independence of the model',r'The dated Fuchs talk starts on P000216 and ends on P000217 before the programme heading. It abstracts the frame and cone constructions by choosing a pointed contractible \(G\)-space and a semigroup of admissible maps. The 1968 Gelfand--Fuchs note gives a recognizable continuation, but strengthens the local semigroup axioms and uses a more restrictive universal-space construction. Those differences prevent an unqualified exact-match verdict for the handwritten theorem.')
item(135,'P000216','definition','A pointed G-space with an admissible semigroup',r'\(S\subset\operatorname{Map}(T,T)\) has the compact-open topology, contains the \(G\)-action, and is stable under left and right multiplication by it. The axioms specify a fixed point, an \(S\)-contraction, faithfulness, closedness of \(G\), and nearby maps that are embeddings.',r'The published elementary \(G\)-object consists of a pointed space \(T\), a topological semigroup \(A\) acting faithfully and fixing the point, an embedded subgroup \(G\), and a contraction through \(A\). Crucially, a neighborhood \(W\supset G\) consists of invertible elements and admits a right-equivariant retraction \(\lambda:W\to G\), with \(\lambda(ag)=\lambda(a)g\) and \(\lambda(g)=g\). This last condition is stronger than merely saying nearby maps are topological embeddings.',r'\citet[\S1, axioms 1--4]{gelfandfuchs1968classifying} is the subsequent formulation, received 17 April 1968 and published that year. Its stronger axiom is necessary to identify what was actually proved.',('gelfandfuchs1968classifying',),cls='A',match='partial',reconstruction=r'The published definition is stated separately from the recorded six-axiom list. No equivalence of the two axiom systems is claimed.')
item(135,'P000216-P000217','construction','Linear and cone elementary objects',r'The examples are \(T=\mathbb R^n\), with all linear maps, and \(T=CG\), with the group action and contractions to the apex. The latter is described as a product of cones with the all-apex point removed.',r'For a closed linear Lie subgroup \(G\), take \(T=\mathbb R^n\) and \(A=\operatorname{End}(\mathbb R^n)\). For a topological group, take the cone of pairs \((g,t)\), \(t\ge0\), with all \((g,0)\) identified, and multiplication \((g,t)(h,s)=(gh,ts)\). The cone example has invertible nonapex elements. The positive-ray example uses multiplication by \(e^u\), with the zero sequence deleted.',r'These are \citet[\S1, Examples 1--2]{gelfandfuchs1968classifying}. The last deleted point is essential to freeness, as in the September ray construction.',('gelfandfuchs1968classifying',),cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(135,'P000217','construction','Universal embeddings versus the published coordinate-invertible model',r'The universal total space is said to be every embedding \(T\to T^I\) whose coordinate maps lie in \(S\). In the linear example this is the frame space.',r'The 1968 construction instead takes tuples \((a_i)\in A^I\) with at least one coordinate invertible in \(A\), equipped with the diagonal right \(G\)-action. Such a tuple gives an embedding \(T\to T^I\), but the converse need not hold: a linear injection into a product can be assembled from singular coordinate maps. Thus the earlier all-frame model and the later coordinate-invertible model must not be identified pointwise.',r'\citet[\S2, Propositions 3--4 and Theorem 1]{gelfandfuchs1968classifying} proves universality for this published model. The all-frame linear model had already been treated in \citet[Proposition 1.1]{gelfandfuchs1967topology}.',('gelfandfuchs1968classifying','gelfandfuchs1967topology'),cls='A',match='partial',reconstruction=r'The distinction is explicit: the 1968 universal object is a related construction, not verbatim the set of all embeddings in the notebook.')
item(135,'P000217','claim','Finite products are finite approximations, not universal EG',r'For finite \(I\), the notes call the linear case the usual classifying space and the cone case Milnor\'s construction with a minor modification.',r'Finite-dimensional Stiefel and Grassmann spaces classify bundles only subject to dimension or embedding restrictions; their total spaces are not generally contractible universal \(EG\) spaces. Likewise, a finite cone-product or finite join is an approximation. Universal models require an appropriate infinite construction and its stated topology, or the sufficiently large index set tailored to the bundle.',r'The large-index quantifier in \citet[Proposition 1.1]{gelfandfuchs1967topology} and \citet[\S2]{gelfandfuchs1968classifying} supplies the precise interpretation. The literal finite-index universal claim is too strong.',('gelfandfuchs1967topology','gelfandfuchs1968classifying'),cls='A',literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction=r'The sentence is interpreted as referring to familiar finite stages, rather than silently changing finite to infinite in the transcription.')
item(135,'P000217','theorem','Local T-triviality and universal pullback representation',r'For each \(x\), the associated \(T\)-bundle has a global map to \(T\), an allowed map on every fiber and a homeomorphism at \(x\). Every bundle over a Hausdorff base maps to the universal one.',r'Under the published elementary-object axioms, require that for every \(x\) there is a continuous associated-bundle map to \(T\) whose restriction, written in any principal frame, belongs to \(A\), and is invertible in \(A\) on the selected fiber. Such bundles are ordinarily locally trivial; ordinary locally trivial bundles over completely regular bases satisfy this condition. Collecting these maps gives the universal morphism for sufficiently large \(I\).',r'\citet[Propositions 1--2 and Theorem 1]{gelfandfuchs1968classifying} proves these statements. Mere homeomorphism as a map of \(T\), without invertibility in the specified semigroup, is not the complete published hypothesis.',('gelfandfuchs1968classifying',),cls='A',match='partial',problem='GP-1967-01',reconstruction=r'The stronger semigroup assumptions and invertibility requirement are retained from the publication.')
item(135,'P000217','theorem','Independence of the elementary object',r'\(H^q_{\mathrm{alg}}(G;\mathbb R)\) does not depend on \(T,S\); the notes add that a group \(A\) may replace \(\mathbb R\).',r'For a paracompact topological group \(G\) and elementary \(G\)-objects satisfying the published axioms, the characteristic groups with coefficients in a fixed topological \(G\)-module are independent of the chosen object. The coefficient sheaf is the sheaf of continuous sections of the associated module bundle. One must hold the coefficient sheaf and the naturality category fixed; the notebook does not specify which arbitrary coefficient groups or sheaves its parenthetical extension includes.',r'\citet[\S3, Theorem 2]{gelfandfuchs1968classifying} is the identifiable continuation. The stronger elementary-object axioms and unspecified coefficient extension make the match partial.',('gelfandfuchs1968classifying',),cls='A',match='partial',problem='GP-1967-01',reconstruction=r'The real-coefficient construction is the definite statement; the unrestricted coefficient parenthesis is not upgraded into an unproved generalization.',confidence='medium')
correction('P000217','Finite-index classifying-space claim','I finite gives the usual classifying space','Finite I gives finite stages; universality requires sufficiently large or infinite models','Finite Stiefel total spaces are not generally contractible. This is a mathematical qualification of the source wording.')

talk(136,'1967-10-02','I. M. Gelfand','P000217-P000220','Programme for future seminar talks',r'The heading asking what will come next occupies the end of P000217 and P000218. It lists Uspenskii on logic; Lidskii on eigenfunction asymptotics after H\"ormander; Sinai on invariant measures; Kazhdan on discrete groups and Selberg\'s problem; Kushnirenko on analytic linearization; Tyurina and S. Gelfand on Pham; Anosov and Maslov; Bernstein on \(P^\lambda\); Gelfand and Graev; S. G. Krein on interpolation; and Berezin and Kirillov on Grassmann analysis. Only the analytic-linearization entry states a sufficiently definite theorem. The programme supplies no dates or additional independent MCCME records for these proposed talks.',kind='plan')
item(136,'P000217','theorem','Analytic linearization of a semisimple action at a fixed point',r'The planned Kushnirenko talk is summarized as: an analytic action of a semisimple group near a fixed point is linear.',r'Let a finite-dimensional real semisimple Lie algebra act by germs of real-analytic vector fields vanishing at a point of a real-analytic manifold. There are local real-analytic coordinates in which the action is its linear isotropy representation. Equivalently, an analytic local action of the corresponding connected group is locally analytically linearizable at the fixed point.',r'\citet[Theorem 1]{kushnirenko1967linearization} proves the result; the note was received 16 November 1966 and published in the first 1967 issue, before this programme. The smooth category cannot simply replace the analytic one.',('kushnirenko1967linearization',),cls='E',reconstruction=r'The local germ formulation and connected-group qualification state the precise theorem behind the one-line announcement.')
item(136,'P000217-P000218','remark','Programme labels that do not specify mathematical assertions',r'The programme mentions conditions for invariant measures, a solution of Selberg\'s problem, Pham\'s work, \(P^\lambda\), Grassmann analysis, and several names without a theorem statement.',r'These entries identify proposed topics and speakers only. In particular, neither the invariant-measure conditions nor which Selberg problem is meant is stated here. No mathematical quantifiers can be recovered solely from these programme labels.',r'The adjacent later records, and the earlier Kazhdan material in 1966-101, provide context but do not turn this plan into a uniquely stated new theorem. No exact publication match is assigned to the topic list.',(),cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',status=r'As of November 2026, this remains a programme record, not an independently identifiable open question. Its named topics are covered in the talk summary and are not counted as invented extra talks.',confidence='high')
save()

talk(138,'1967-10-16','Ya. G. Sinai','P000220-P000224','Toral automorphisms, entropy, and symbolic Markov models',r'The Sinai heading begins at the bottom of P000221, after the interpolation discussion, and ends on P000223 before Leray. The exact model and recoding are those of Adler and Weiss, already published in June 1967. The general finite Markov-shift parenthesis goes beyond that special proof and leads to the later Bernoulli isomorphism theory. The continuation on 30 October explains geometric Markov partitions.')
item(138,'P000221','theorem','Hyperbolic toral automorphisms and Lebesgue spectrum',r'The integer matrix defines a two-torus map. Irrational eigenvalues are said to imply real eigenvalues, ergodicity, and countably multiple Lebesgue spectrum.',r'Let \(A\in SL(2,\mathbb Z)\) satisfy \(|\operatorname{tr}A|>2\). Its toral automorphism preserves Haar probability and is ergodic. Its Koopman operator on the mean-zero subspace of \(L^2(\mathbb T^2)\) is a countable direct sum of bilateral shifts; the constant functions separately contribute the eigenvalue \(1\). Indeed, the Fourier characters indexed by nonzero lattice points split into infinite \(A^{\mathsf T}\)-orbits. Nonrational eigenvalues alone do not imply real eigenvalues: a finite-order rotation matrix is a counterexample.',r'This is the hyperbolic toral setting of \citet[\S1]{adlerweiss1967entropy}. The Fourier-orbit argument supplies the precise spectral meaning of the notebook sentence; the hyperbolicity hypothesis and removal of constants are indispensable.',('adlerweiss1967entropy',),literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction=r'The statement uses the intended real hyperbolic case; it does not redefine irrational to mean real without saying so.')
item(138,'P000221-P000222','problem','Entropy classifies hyperbolic two-torus automorphisms metrically',r'When does a measurable \(U\) satisfy \(UT_1=T_2U\)? The entropy is \(\log\lambda\), where \(\lambda>1\), and equal entropy is asserted to imply metric similarity, although integral linear conjugacy can fail.',r'For hyperbolic automorphisms \(T_A,T_B\) of \(\mathbb T^2\), with Haar probability, there is a measure-preserving invertible conjugacy modulo null sets if and only if their entropies agree. Here \(h(T_A)=\log\rho(A)\). Equality of entropy does not generally imply conjugacy of the matrices in \(GL(2,\mathbb Z)\). Merely requiring a measurable map, without invertibility and preservation of measure, is a different and much weaker question.',r'This is exactly the Main Theorem of \citet[\S1]{adlerweiss1967entropy}, communicated on 24 April 1967 and published in June 1967. It was therefore already known at the October lecture, although the notes present its proof programme.',('adlerweiss1967entropy',),problem='GP-1967-03',reconstruction=r'Metric similarity is interpreted as isomorphism of probability-preserving transformations modulo zero; \(\lambda\) is the modulus of the expanding eigenvalue.',status=r'As of November 2026, the stated two-dimensional classification is settled. This occurrence is not an open entropy-classification problem for arbitrary measure-preserving systems.')
item(138,'P000222','claim','Topological conjugacies are affine under ergodicity',r'The notes say that if \(U\) is a homeomorphism then it is linear, adding in parentheses that this holds for any compact abelian groups.',r'If \(A,B\in GL(n,\mathbb Z)\), \(T_A\) is ergodic, and a homeomorphism \(U\) satisfies \(UT_A=T_BU\), then \(U(x)=Cx+c\), with \(C\in GL(n,\mathbb Z)\), \(CA=BC\), and \(T_Bc=c\). Thus \(U\) is linear after imposing \(U(0)=0\). Without ergodicity even the identity transformation admits many nonlinear conjugacies. The unrestricted compact-abelian parenthesis lacks its necessary hypotheses.',r'\citet[Theorem, pp.1223--1225]{adlerpalais1965homeomorphic} proves precisely the toral affine-rigidity assertion. The paper was received in November 1964 and published in 1965. Its proof does not justify deleting the translation term.',('adlerpalais1965homeomorphic',),literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction=r'The verified theorem is restricted to tori and states the translation term explicitly; the general compact-group extension is not asserted.')
item(138,'P000222','definition','A primitive two-sided shift of finite type',r'\(\Omega_\Pi\) consists of bi-infinite sequences \((i_n)\) with \(\pi_{i_ni_{n+1}}=1\), for a finite zero-one matrix. A power of \(\Pi\) is assumed strictly positive.',r'For a finite zero-one matrix \(A\), let \(\Sigma_A=\{x\in\{1,\ldots,s\}^{\mathbb Z}:A_{x_nx_{n+1}}=1\}\), with the product topology and the coordinate shift \(\sigma\). Primitivity means \(A^k>0\) for some \(k\); it implies topological mixing of this shift. The Borel sigma algebra is generated by finite-coordinate cylinders.',r'\citet[\S\S2--3]{adlerweiss1967entropy} and \citet[\S\S2--4]{parry1964intrinsic} use this exact finite-state setup. The cylinder sigma algebra mentioned later in the notebook is part of this definition.',('adlerweiss1967entropy','parry1964intrinsic'),literal='correct')
item(138,'P000222','construction','The identity, full-shift, and golden-mean examples',r'The three matrices are \(I_2\), the two-by-two all-ones matrix, and \(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&1\\1&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)\). The last is left for examination.',r'For \(I_2\), the shift space consists of two fixed sequences and the matrix is not primitive. The all-ones matrix gives the full two-shift, with entropy \(\log2\). The third matrix forbids consecutive occurrences of symbol \(2\); it is primitive, has Perron eigenvalue \(\varphi=(1+\sqrt5)/2\), and has entropy \(\log\varphi\).',r'These elementary examples follow directly from the finite-state framework and Perron construction in \citet[Theorems 5 and 7]{parry1964intrinsic}. The last example is worked out here as an annotation to the instruction to examine it.',('parry1964intrinsic',),literal='correct',reconstruction=r'The forbidden word and entropy of the third matrix are an editorial calculation.')
item(138,'P000222','construction','The Parry transition probabilities need both Perron vectors',r'The notebook writes \(\lambda e=\Pi^*e\), then \(p_{ij}=e_j\pi_{ij}/(\lambda e_i)\), and uses \(e_i\) as the stationary weights in the entropy formula.',r'Let \(A\) be primitive, \(Ar=\lambda r\), \(l^{\mathsf T}A=\lambda l^{\mathsf T}\), with positive vectors normalized by \(l^{\mathsf T}r=1\). Then \(P_{ij}=A_{ij}r_j/(\lambda r_i)\) is stochastic and \(w_i=l_ir_i\) is stationary. The cylinder probability is \(w_{i_0}P_{i_0i_1}\cdots P_{i_{m-1}i_m}\). A left Perron vector cannot replace \(r\) in the displayed row-transition formula for a general nonsymmetric matrix.',r'The correct construction is \citet[Theorem 5]{parry1964intrinsic}, also presented in \citet[\S3]{adlerweiss1967entropy}. Both earlier publications distinguish the two eigenvectors. A nonsymmetric numerical example is recorded in this fragment\'s computation file.',('parry1964intrinsic','adlerweiss1967entropy'),literal='false as written',reconstruction=r'The transpose is removed from the right-eigenvector equation, and the separate stationary vector \(w\) replaces the reused letter \(e\).')
item(138,'P000222','theorem','Entropy and uniqueness of the maximal stationary measure',r'The entropy of a stationary Markov chain is displayed as a weighted sum of row entropies. The preceding chain is said to have entropy \(\log\lambda\) and to be the unique measure of maximal entropy.',r'For a finite stationary chain with distribution \(w\) and transition matrix \(P\), \(h_\mu(\sigma)=-\sum_iw_i\sum_jP_{ij}\log P_{ij}\), with \(0\log0=0\). For the Parry chain above the coboundary terms \(\log r_j-\log r_i\) cancel by stationarity, leaving \(\log\lambda\). An irreducible finite shift of finite type has this unique invariant probability of maximal entropy; primitivity additionally gives mixing.',r'\citet[Theorems 7 and 9]{parry1964intrinsic} proves the entropy and uniqueness results. The notebook\'s maximization must range over shift-invariant probabilities, rather than all measures on the cylinder sigma algebra.',('parry1964intrinsic',),reconstruction=r'The correct stationary weights, invariance restriction, and distinction between irreducibility and primitivity are supplied.')
item(138,'P000222','theorem','Gelfand on second-order minors and one sign change',r'Gelfand remarks that if all entries and all second-order minors are positive, the first eigenvector is positive and the second has one sign change. He points to the action on the second exterior power.',r'For a real \(n\times n\) matrix, \(n\ge2\), with all entries and all ordered two-by-two minors strictly positive, the two eigenvalues of largest modulus are simple and satisfy \(\lambda_1>\lambda_2>0\), with \(\lambda_2>|\lambda_j|\) for \(j\ge3\). A first eigenvector is strictly positive; a second has exactly one sign variation after zero coordinates are omitted. Perron\'s theorem applied to the matrix and its second compound gives the spectral assertion. Positivity of \(v_1\wedge v_2\) makes the ratios \(v_{2i}/v_{1i}\) strictly monotone, and orthogonality to a positive left Perron vector forces their signs to cross.',r'This is an elementary second-compound specialization of the oscillation method of \citet[\S\S4--5, pp.460--466]{gantmacherkrein1937matrices}. The exterior-power proof specified in the notebook is recognizable; no assumption of positivity of all higher minors is needed for this two-eigenvector conclusion.',('gantmacherkrein1937matrices',),literal='correct',match='partial',reconstruction=r'The statement separates the precise second-order consequence from the stronger full oscillation theorems, and makes the handling of zero coordinates explicit.',speaker='I. M. Gelfand')
item(138,'P000223','construction','The four-block symbolic matrix',r'The alphabet has groups of sizes \(p,q,r,s\). From either \(a\)-group one may move to \(a^1\) or \(b^1\); from either \(b\)-group to \(a^2\) or \(b^2\). The equation is \(\lambda^2-(p+s)\lambda+ps-qr=0\), with \(ps-qr=1\).',r'Order the symbols as \(a^1,a^2,b^1,b^2\). Give all rows in the first two groups the allowed columns \(a^1,b^1\), and all rows in the last two groups the allowed columns \(a^2,b^2\). For positive block sizes the resulting matrix is primitive, has rank two, and has characteristic polynomial \(t^{p+q+r+s-2}(t^2-(p+s)t+ps-qr)\). This reduction follows by restricting to vectors constant on the two row types.',r'The construction and characteristic polynomial occur exactly in \citet[\S4]{adlerweiss1967entropy}. The Greek/Roman ambiguity in the notebook\'s transition labels does not affect the four named blocks or their destinations.',('adlerweiss1967entropy',),literal='correct',reconstruction=r'Positivity of the four sizes is imposed for the simple primitive case. Zero-sized or sign-modified geometric cases need their separate conventions.')
item(138,'P000223','theorem','Recoding to a full matrix with one forbidden transition',r'The second model has \(N\) symbols and every transition except \(N\to1\). The two shifts are called isomorphic, and the coding is said to carry one maximal measure to the other.',r'Let the positive four-block matrix satisfy \(ps-qr=1\), and set \(N=p+s\). With their respective Parry measures, its shift and the shift defined by \(J_N-E_{N1}\) are measurably isomorphic modulo zero. The second matrix has characteristic polynomial \(t^{N-2}(t^2-Nt+1)\), so the Perron roots and entropies agree. The isomorphism is a variable-block measurable coding, not a global topological conjugacy.',r'This is precisely the determinant-one theorem in \citet[\S4]{adlerweiss1967entropy}. The paper proves the almost-everywhere invertible block coding and preservation of the maximal measures; equality of the eigenvalues alone would not prove it.',('adlerweiss1967entropy',),problem='GP-1967-03',reconstruction=r'The category of isomorphism and the relation \(N=p+s\) are made explicit. Transposing the single forbidden transition merely changes the symbol/reversal convention.')
item(138,'P000223','claim','The parenthetical extension to all equal-entropy Markov shifts',r'A parenthesis refers to isomorphism of any two \(\Omega_\Pi\) with equal entropy, without another proof.',r'For primitive finite matrices, equip each two-sided shift with its Parry probability. These mixing finite-state Markov systems are Bernoulli, and two are measure-theoretically isomorphic exactly when their entropies agree. This is a statement about probability-preserving systems; equality of entropy does not classify shifts of finite type up to topological conjugacy.',r'The general claim is supplied by the later Bernoulli theory: the 1970 Friedman--Ornstein Markov result and Ornstein\'s equal-entropy isomorphism theorem are explicitly presented together in \citet[\S5, Theorems 4--5 and references 3, 5]{ornstein1975applications}. That primary author exposition was read; the 1970 original proof was not accessible in this audit. Adler--Weiss\'s June 1967 proof verifies the preceding special family only.',('ornstein1975applications','adlerweiss1967entropy'),cls='B',match='partial',reconstruction=r'The notebook parenthesis is interpreted in its surrounding maximal-measure and primitive-matrix setting.',status=r'As of November 2026, the qualified measurable statement is established. No unrestricted entropy-only topological classification is asserted.')
correction('P000221','Toral eigenvalue implication','Irrational eigenvalues imply real eigenvalues','For A in SL(2,Z), impose |tr A|>2 for the real hyperbolic case','Finite-order integral matrices can have nonrational nonreal eigenvalues; the scan faithfully records the compressed implication.')
correction('P000222','Homeomorphic conjugacy','U is linear for any compact abelian groups','For ergodic toral automorphisms U is affine; U(0)=0 removes its translation','Adler--Palais1965 theorem states the affine term and ergodicity hypothesis. The general compact-group parenthesis is underspecified.')
correction('P000222','Perron vector and Markov probabilities',r'\lambda e=\Pi^*e; p_{ij}=e_j\pi_{ij}/(\lambda e_i)',r'\Pi r=\lambda r; p_{ij}=\pi_{ij}r_j/(\lambda r_i); w_i=l_ir_i','The scan clearly has a star. Right row-transition normalization requires a right eigenvector and stationary weights require both Perron vectors.')
save()

talk(139,'1967-10-23','Jean Leray','P000222-P000225','Algebraic singular supports of parameter-dependent periods',r'The Leray heading begins near the bottom of P000223 and ends midway through P000224, before the date 30 October. Its relative cycles, moving hyperplanes, higher residues, and algebraic singular-support theorem match Leray\'s 1967 complement to Nilsson. The singular support is algebraic; the period function itself need not be algebraic. The notebook does not state the separate slow-growth conjecture appearing in Leray\'s paper.')
item(139,'P000223','definition','Projective relative periods',r'\(J(t)=\int_{\gamma^q(t)}\omega(t,\xi)\), with \(t\in\mathbb C^n\), \(\xi\in\mathbb C^{\ell+1}\), a cycle relative to \(W(t)\), and a degree-zero homogeneous form vanishing on \(W(t)\). Homogeneity is said to put the form on \(\mathbb P^\ell\).',r'A differential form on \(\mathbb C^{\ell+1}\setminus\{0\}\) descends to projective space when it is invariant under scalar multiplication and horizontal for the Euler vector field. For a closed relative \(q\)-form \(\omega_t\) with zero pullback to \(W_t\), integrate over a relative real \(q\)-cycle with boundary in \(W_t\), avoiding the polar locus. The period depends on its relative homology class, and a local family of cycles must be transported continuously away from singular parameters.',r'This is the setup of \citet[\S1, pp.313--315]{leray1967complement}. The projective-basic condition, closedness, and relative-cycle interpretation make precise the notebook\'s compressed notation.',('leray1967complement',),cls='E',match='partial',reconstruction=r'Invariance alone is insufficient for a general differential form; horizontality and closedness are explicit editorial hypotheses.')
item(139,'P000224','construction','The degeneracy locus of a moving hyperplane arrangement',r'\(W(t)=\bigcup_kW_k(t)\) is a union of hyperplanes depending algebraically on \(t\). The set \(S_0[W]\) is where the dimension of an intersection changes.',r'For a finite algebraic family of projective hyperplanes, the ranks of the matrices defining every subfamily are generically constant. Their rank-drop conditions, given by minors, produce an algebraic discriminant after coefficients and branch choices are placed on the appropriate algebraic parameter space. Outside it the incidence type is locally constant. Singularities involving the polar variety require additional tangency or stratified nontransversality conditions, not only hyperplane intersection ranks.',r'\citet[\S1 and \S\S3--4]{leray1967complement} describes the arrangement singular set and the additional appui conditions. The notebook\'s dimension-change test names only the arrangement part of the full exceptional locus.',('leray1967complement',),cls='A',match='partial',reconstruction=r'Generic rank and algebraic-branch qualifications are supplied; the arrangement discriminant is not equated with all possible period singularities.')
item(139,'P000224','method','Iterated residues and the cycle on the complete intersection',r'The notes identify \(\gamma^{q-m}(t)\) with \(\bigcap_jV_j(t)\), write poles \(v_j^{-1-\beta_j}\), and use a quotient of derivatives by powers of \(dv_j\) as a residue notation.',r'For transverse smooth hypersurfaces \(V_j=\{v_j=0\}\) and a meromorphic \(q\)-form \(\eta\) with simple logarithmic poles, its iterated residue is a \((q-m)\)-form on their complete intersection. A real \((q-m)\)-cycle \(\gamma\) there has an iterated tubular \(q\)-cycle \(\delta^m\gamma\), and, with compatible orientations, \(\int_{\delta^m\gamma}\eta=(2\pi i)^m\int_\gamma\operatorname{Res}_{V_1,\ldots,V_m}\eta\). Higher pole orders are handled by Leray residue classes and pole-order differentiation with the corresponding factorials. Powers of a one-form in the notebook denominator are not ordinary exterior powers.',r'The exact higher-pole notation and its reduction to a tubular integral occur in \citet[\S1, formula (3), and \S20, p.352]{leray1967complement}. The published cycle lies on the complete intersection; it is not identical to that algebraic variety.',('leray1967complement',),cls='A',literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction=r'The simple-pole residue identity states a precise special case. The higher-pole notebook expression is retained as symbolic residue notation, not expanded into an unjustified differential quotient.')
item(139,'P000224','theorem','Algebraic singular support of the period',r'The theorem says that \(J(t)\) has algebraic singular support: it is holomorphic on a covering of the complement of an algebraic set. Leray describes that set in the hyperplane case.',r'In Leray\'s projective relative-period setting, assume the integrand has algebraic singular support and impose his non-appui hypothesis for the moving arrangement against the polar and auxiliary algebraic varieties. Then there is an algebraic hypersurface \(S\) in parameter space such that the analytically continued period is holomorphic on the universal covering of its complement. Its singular support lies in the union of the arrangement discriminant and the specified incidence/tangency locus. This permits nontrivial monodromy and transcendental periods.',r'This is \citet[Theorem 1, pp.316--317]{leray1967complement}; its Corollary 1.1 identifies the absolute-cycle case with Nilsson\'s earlier theorem. The publisher dates \citet{nilsson1964growth} to October 1964, despite later bibliographies sometimes using the volume year 1965. Leray\'s general relative statement appeared in 1967. Only the absolute-case attribution, not Nilsson\'s full proof, was independently checked in the latter source.',('leray1967complement','nilsson1964growth'),cls='A',match='partial',reconstruction=r'The omitted non-appui hypothesis is retained by name and its role is explained; the algebraicity assertion concerns the exceptional set, not the value of the integral.',status=r'As of November 2026, the stated singular-support theorem is established. The notes do not pose a slow-growth question, and none is manufactured from the separate hypothetical Theorem 3 in Leray\'s paper.')
correction('P000223','Projective descent','The form is on projective space because it is homogeneous','A projective differential form must be invariant under scalars and horizontal','For forms of positive degree, zero total homogeneity by itself need not imply descent. Leray works with forms on projective space.')
correction('P000224','Residue cycle',r'\gamma^{q-m}=\bigcap_{j=1}^m V_j',r'\gamma^{q-m} is a real cycle on the complete intersection','The scan shows an equality/cycle expression; Leray1967 section1 places the cycle on the intersection. Its dimension is a homological dimension, not the complex dimension of the entire variety.')
save()

talk(140,'1967-10-30','Ya. G. Sinai','P000223-P000230','Anosov diffeomorphisms and geometric Markov partitions',r'The actual dated heading starts midway through P000224 and the talk ends on P000228. P000229 begins a new calendar-year block and does not belong to this talk despite the catalogue overlap. Stable and unstable foliations lead to rectangles, Markov coding, leaf measures, and the two-torus entropy proof. The first Sinai paper was received on 20 October 1967, ten days before this continuation; the construction paper followed on 3 November. The algebraic-classification question repeats GP-1966-01.')
item(140,'P000224-P000225','definition','Uniform hyperbolicity and the compact-distance caveat',r'The \(U\)-condition is illustrated by exponential separation of nearby points in one time direction, followed by contracting and expanding submanifolds of dimensions \(k,\ell\), with \(k+\ell=n\) and uniform transversality.',r'A \(C^1\) diffeomorphism \(T\) of a compact Riemannian manifold is Anosov if \(TM=E^s\oplus E^u\) is a continuous invariant splitting and there are \(C>0\), \(0<\theta<1\) such that \(\|DT^nv_s\|\le C\theta^n\|v_s\|\) and \(\|DT^{-n}v_u\|\le C\theta^n\|v_u\|\) for all \(n\ge0\). Uniform exponential expansion is a derivative or leafwise local statement. Distances between two fixed distinct points in a compact space cannot grow exponentially for all time.',r'The standard splitting and its stable/unstable interpretation are recalled by \citet[\S1]{sinai1968markov} and, in modern notation, \citet[Definition 2.1]{spatzier2016rigidity}. These were the established Anosov framework by the lecture, not a new consequence of the displayed metric inequality.',('sinai1968markov','spatzier2016rigidity'),literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction=r'The derivative definition replaces the globally impossible distance inequality. One may also use intrinsic leaf metrics as in Sinai\'s publication.')
item(140,'P000224-P000225','theorem','Stable manifolds and the choice of contraction constants',r'Points with exponentially shrinking forward distance form \(\Gamma^{(c)}(x)\); backward shrinking gives \(\Gamma^{(p)}(x)\). The sets are said not to depend on \(a,c\).',r'For an Anosov diffeomorphism, \(W^s(x)=\{y:d(T^ny,T^nx)\to0\text{ as }n\to+\infty\}\) and the analogous \(W^u(x)\) are immersed leaves tangent to \(E^s,E^u\), of complementary dimensions. Their local plaques vary continuously and are uniformly transverse. For \(C^r\) dynamics the individual leaves have the corresponding smoothness, although transverse regularity is weaker. Changing to admissible weaker exponential estimates does not change the global asymptotic leaf; a fixed local plaque or a prescribed overly fast rate can change the selected set.',r'\citet[\S1, pp.64--65]{sinai1968markov} explicitly distinguishes leafwise metrics and allowable contraction constants. The notebook\'s independence of constants is valid only in that standard asymptotic sense.',('sinai1968markov',),match='partial',reconstruction=r'Global leaves, local plaques, and the range of admissible exponential rates are distinguished.')
item(140,'P000225','construction','Linear examples and structural stability',r'An integral two-by-two matrix of determinant one is given as an example. Smooth periodic nonlinear perturbations remain \(U\)-diffeomorphisms and have the same rotation number; a later sentence attributes topological conjugacy under small perturbations to Anosov.',r'A toral map \(T_A\), \(A\in GL(n,\mathbb Z)\), is Anosov precisely when no eigenvalue has modulus one. Every sufficiently small \(C^1\) perturbation is Anosov and is topologically conjugate to \(T_A\); more generally every Anosov diffeomorphism is structurally stable in this sense. In dimension two, determinant one alone is insufficient. The stable and unstable asymptotic directions supply the intended rotation/slope information, but there is no single circle rotation number for an arbitrary toral map.',r'The structural-stability theorem and algebraic examples are part of \citet{smale1967dynamical}; the precise modern theorem is stated in \citet[Theorem 2.2]{spatzier2016rigidity}. The notebook itself correctly attributes the perturbation result to Anosov.',('smale1967dynamical','spatzier2016rigidity'),match='partial',reconstruction=r'Smallness is in \(C^1\), hyperbolicity is explicit, and the informal rotation-number phrase is not promoted to a new invariant.')
item(140,'P000225','construction','Lattice-preserving Lie-group automorphisms',r'An automorphism of a Lie group preserves a discrete cocompact subgroup and descends to the quotient. It is \(U\) if its Lie-algebra map has no eigenvalue of modulus one.',r'Let \(G\) be a simply connected Lie group with lattice \(\Gamma\), and let \(\Phi\in\operatorname{Aut}(G)\) satisfy \(\Phi(\Gamma)=\Gamma\). Its induced diffeomorphism of \(G/\Gamma\) is Anosov when \(d\Phi_e\) has no eigenvalue on the unit circle; the invariant tangent framing makes this criterion necessary as well. Nilpotent \(G\) gives the standard nilmanifold algebraic examples.',r'The algebraic construction is described in \citet[algebraic examples]{smale1967dynamical} and is the model class in \citet[\S2]{spatzier2016rigidity}. The group, lattice-preservation, and compactness conditions are integral to the statement.',('smale1967dynamical','spatzier2016rigidity'),literal='correct',match='partial')
item(140,'P000225','claim','Nilpotence does not guarantee an Anosov lattice automorphism',r'The parenthesis states that such automorphisms exist if and only if the Lie algebra is nilpotent.',r'Nilpotence alone is not sufficient. For the three-dimensional Heisenberg group with its standard lattice, a lattice-preserving automorphism induces \(B\in GL(2,\mathbb Z)\) on the abelianization and acts on the one-dimensional center by \(\det B=\pm1\). Hence its derivative always has an eigenvalue of modulus one and cannot define an Anosov automorphism. The existence of an appropriate lattice-preserving hyperbolic automorphism is an additional arithmetic and algebraic condition.',r'The restricted nature of algebraic examples is consistent with \citet[\S2]{spatzier2016rigidity}. The explicit Heisenberg bracket calculation is supplied here as a direct counterexample to the notebook\'s converse; no general classification of Anosov Lie algebras is claimed.',('spatzier2016rigidity',),literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction=r'This is an editorial counterexample, with its symbolic bracket calculation included in the fragment\'s checks.')
item(140,'P000225','problem','Are all Anosov diffeomorphisms algebraic up to conjugacy?',r'The main problem is \ru{топ. подобность алгебраич.}; small perturbations are already handled by Anosov\'s theorem.',r'Is every Anosov diffeomorphism of a compact connected manifold topologically conjugate to an affine Anosov automorphism of an infranilmanifold? The distinction between determining the underlying manifold and conjugating its dynamics is essential. Smooth conjugacy is a stronger assertion and is not the question recorded here.',r'This repeats 1966-94.4 and reuses GP-1966-01. \citet[Conjecture 2.3 and Theorem 2.4]{spatzier2016rigidity} states the precise conjecture and the Franks--Manning result on infranilmanifolds. \citet[\S2]{damjanovic2026symmetries} still describes algebraicity of general Anosov dynamics as conjectural. Sinai\'s two-torus case appears as Theorem 5.2 of \citet{sinai1968markov}.',('spatzier2016rigidity','damjanovic2026symmetries','sinai1968markov'),cls='C',problem='GP-1966-01',match='exact',literal='correct',status=r'As of November 2026, the general classification remains open. The two-torus case and the Franks--Manning classification when the manifold is already an infranilmanifold are established; structural stability solves only a neighborhood of a known example.',worth=r'Further work is worthwhile on explicit manifold classes or additional dynamical hypotheses, since these address the remaining global classification rather than reproving local structural stability.')
item(140,'P000225','conjecture','Hyperbolicity on the nonwandering set: an incomplete quantifier',r'The manifold is split into wandering and nonwandering points, and the \(U\)-condition is asserted on the latter, with a margin note saying this too is a hypothesis.',r'For a homeomorphism, a point is nonwandering when every neighborhood meets one of its positive iterates; its complement is the wandering set. It is false that the nonwandering set of every smooth diffeomorphism is hyperbolic: the identity is an immediate counterexample. A density or genericity conjecture would require a topology, a class of systems, and an approximation quantifier, none of which is stated here.',r'The terminology belongs to the hyperbolic-dynamics programme surveyed by \citet{smale1967dynamical}, but this fragment does not determine which conjecture was intended. It must not be automatically identified with the earlier Axiom A or periodic-density questions merely because the words nonwandering and hyperbolic occur.',('smale1967dynamical',),cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='indirect',status=r'As of November 2026, the literal universal assertion is false and the intended qualified conjecture cannot be uniquely recovered. No new problem-index entry is assigned.',confidence='medium')
item(140,'P000226','theorem','Absolute continuity of holonomy',r'The canonical map between nearby stable plaques, obtained along unstable plaques, need not be smooth but is absolutely continuous; natural measures are said to be carried into one another.',r'For a \(C^{1+\alpha}\) Anosov diffeomorphism, \(\alpha>0\), stable and unstable holonomies between appropriate smooth transverse plaques are absolutely continuous with respect to the induced Riemannian volume classes. Thus a null set is carried to a null set in both directions. This does not assert equality of the two Riemannian volume measures or differentiability of the holonomy.',r'\citet[\S1, p.65]{sinai1968markov} states exactly the equivalence of the induced volume classes, attributing the established argument to Anosov and noting that a smooth invariant global measure is not required. The notebook\'s warning about lack of smoothness is substantive.',('sinai1968markov',),cls='E',reconstruction=r'The modern \(C^{1+\alpha}\) regularity condition and preservation of measure classes, rather than measure values, are explicit.')
item(140,'P000226','definition','Rectangles from the local product structure',r'A parallelogram consists of stable and unstable leaf pieces, canonically identified within each family. Its boundary splits into expanding and contracting parts; finitely many such pieces cover the manifold with disjoint interiors.',r'A sufficiently small rectangle is a set closed under the local bracket \([x,y]=W^s_{\mathrm{loc}}(x)\cap W^u_{\mathrm{loc}}(y)\), with a product description by stable and unstable plaques. A rectangular partition has finitely many such closed pieces, covers the manifold, and has pairwise disjoint interiors. Its two boundary types come from the boundaries in the two local-product factors. In higher dimensions these rectangles need not be Euclidean polyhedra.',r'This is \citet[\S1, Definitions 1.1--1.2]{sinai1968markov}; the published admissibility conditions also control plaque-boundary measure. The schematic figures in the notebook do not assert piecewise linear boundaries.',('sinai1968markov',),cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'The local bracket and non-Euclidean meaning of rectangle are made explicit.')
item(140,'P000226','definition','Markov boundary inclusions',r'The partition is Markov when, for some \(m>0\), \(\Gamma_p(T^{-m}\alpha)\subset\Gamma_p(\alpha)\) and \(\Gamma_c(T^m\alpha)\subset\Gamma_c(\alpha)\).',r'For a sufficiently small rectangular partition, use the notebook\'s stable/unstable boundary conventions and require those two inclusions. In the usual one-step formulation, if \(x\in\operatorname{int}R_i\) and \(Tx\in\operatorname{int}R_j\), then \(T(W^s(x,R_i))\subset W^s(Tx,R_j)\) and \(T(W^u(x,R_i))\supset W^u(Tx,R_j)\). The direction of inclusion is essential.',r'\citet[Definition 1.3]{sinai1968markov} gives the same iterate-based boundary definition. \citet[Definitions 1.1 and 2.2]{sinai1968construction} uses it in the existence proof.',('sinai1968markov','sinai1968construction'),cls='A',literal='correct',reconstruction=r'The plaque formulation supplies a coordinate-free interpretation while retaining the recorded boundary convention.')
item(140,'P000226','theorem','Existence of arbitrarily fine Markov partitions',r'The technical theorem asserts many Markov partitions: each given rectangular partition can be approximated within any \(\varepsilon\), allowing an iterate \(m\). The figures constrain how endpoints lie on opposite boundaries.',r'Every Anosov diffeomorphism of a compact manifold has Markov partitions with arbitrarily small diameters. The construction starts with controlled local-product coverings, adjusts stable and unstable boundary pieces by convergent displacements, and forms a compatible partition. An unrestricted distance on every possible preassigned partition is not specified by the notebook and should not be inferred from the small-mesh theorem alone.',r'The theorem is announced in \citet[\S1, p.66]{sinai1968markov}, received 20 October 1967. The detailed proof is \citet[\S1 in dimension two; \S2 in general]{sinai1968construction}, received 3 November 1967 and published in issue 3 of 1968. Its controlled covering-distance construction explains the stronger approximation language in the talk, without defining the notebook\'s unspecified metric on all partitions.',('sinai1968markov','sinai1968construction'),cls='A',match='partial',reconstruction=r'The definite proved small-mesh theorem is separated from the informal universal approximation wording.')
item(140,'P000226-P000227','construction','Reducing an iterate-Markov partition to one step',r'Take \(\alpha_1=\alpha\vee T^{-1}\alpha\vee\cdots\vee T^{-m+1}\alpha\); the union of transformed boundary pieces shows that \(m=1\) now suffices.',r'If the rectangular partition is Markov for the boundary inclusions at iterate \(m\), its indicated common refinement, interpreted by the local-product components of nonempty intersections, satisfies the one-step inclusions. The boundary is contained in the union of the finitely many transformed boundaries, so the iterate condition closes the last term under one further iterate.',r'This construction is stated explicitly in \citet[\S1, immediately after the existence theorem]{sinai1968markov}. It is the same refinement written across the notebook page break.',('sinai1968markov',),cls='A',literal='correct',reconstruction=r'Taking the relevant nonempty local-product components avoids treating an arbitrary disconnected intersection as a single small chart.')
item(140,'P000227','method','Coding by admissible itineraries',r'\(\pi_{ij}=1\) when \(V_i\cap TV_j\ne\varnothing\). An admissible sequence is mapped to \(\bigcap_nT^n\overline V_{j_n}\), a single point for a sufficiently fine partition. Boundary points can have several codes; uniqueness holds on a set of second category.',r'Using open rectangle interiors in the transition rule, a sufficiently fine Markov partition gives a continuous surjection from a two-sided shift of finite type onto the manifold. The intersection of the closed itinerary rectangles is one point, and the coding intertwines the shift with \(T\) or \(T^{-1}\) according to the time-index convention. Off the union of all iterates of partition boundaries the itinerary is unique; this is a residual set when the boundaries are nowhere dense. Global injectivity is not asserted.',r'This is the construction and conclusion of \citet[\S3, Theorem 3.1]{sinai1968markov}. The notebook already recognizes the boundary obstruction, so replacing its coding by a homeomorphism would lose a genuine qualification.',('sinai1968markov',),cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'The interiors and time orientation are specified; nonempty intersections caused only by shared closed boundaries are not automatically allowed transitions.')
item(140,'P000227','theorem','Primitivity and density of the invariant leaves',r'A strictly positive power of \(\Pi\) is required, and this is said to be equivalent to density of every stable and unstable leaf.',r'Within the Markov coding of a connected Anosov system, the mixing case has a primitive transition matrix. Sinai\'s theorem identifies this condition with density of every stable and every unstable leaf. His word transitive in this part of the paper is defined by the positive-power matrix condition, which is stronger than mere irreducibility of a general finite matrix. It is an additional dynamical hypothesis, not part of the definition of every Anosov diffeomorphism.',r'\citet[\S3, Definition 3.2 and Theorem 3.2]{sinai1968markov} gives the recorded equivalence. The precise published convention prevents accidental conflation of periodic irreducible chains with primitive ones.',('sinai1968markov',),cls='A',reconstruction=r'The original terminology is translated into primitivity/mixing and kept separate from the unresolved general transitivity questions.')
item(140,'P000227','construction','Holonomy-invariant measures on stable and unstable leaves',r'Each stable leaf has a sigma-finite measure invariant under canonical holonomy, with \(\lambda_c(TA)=h\lambda_c(A)\), \(h<1\), an algebraic number. The finite-dimensional construction is \(h^{-1}\vec\lambda=\Pi\vec\lambda\).',r'For the mixing Anosov system under discussion, there are nonzero locally finite, hence sigma-finite, stable-leaf measures invariant under unstable holonomy, with \(\mu^s(TA)=\lambda^{-1}\mu^s(A)\), where \(\lambda>1\) is the Perron eigenvalue of a one-step Markov matrix. The analogous unstable-leaf measures scale by \(\lambda\) under \(T\). Initial plaque masses satisfy the appropriate Perron eigenvector equation, and subdivision extends them to the leaves. Thus the scaling factors are algebraic. They need not be equivalent to Riemannian leaf volume.',r'This is exactly \citet[\S5, Theorem 5.1, pp.84--86]{sinai1968markov}; it is a general statement in that mixing setting, preceding the two-torus specialization in Theorem 5.2. For linear toral examples the leaf measures arise from Haar measure, as the notebook remarks.',('sinai1968markov',),cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'The notebook\'s \(h\) is \(\lambda^{-1}\), a scaling factor rather than entropy. The separate unstable family and singularity possibility are made explicit.')
item(140,'P000227','theorem','An invariant measure with a finite Markov model',r'As a corollary there is an invariant measure for which \(T\) is a finite Markov chain. The illustrated construction then specializes to dimension two.',r'For a mixing finite Markov coding, the Parry probability pushes forward to an invariant probability. When the coding boundaries have zero measure, the resulting system is isomorphic modulo zero to the finite-state stationary Markov shift. For a hyperbolic toral automorphism, Haar probability has exactly this Markov property and is the maximal-entropy measure. For nonlinear dynamics the constructed maximal-entropy measure need not be smooth volume or the measure with absolutely continuous unstable conditionals.',r'\citet[Theorem 5.4 and its proof]{sinai1968markov} proves the Haar/Markov assertion for every hyperbolic toral automorphism; Theorem 5.1 supplies the leaf-measure construction used in the surrounding talk. The boundary-null and measure-class distinctions explain what is required when the notebook states its broader corollary without qualifications.',('sinai1968markov','parry1964intrinsic'),cls='A',match='partial',reconstruction=r'The precise verified toral theorem is separated from the general coding implication. Maximal-entropy measure is not silently identified with the different invariant measure constructed from smooth unstable conditionals.')
item(140,'P000228','theorem','Entropy, the Perron root, and the repeated metric classification',r'The page first gives \(h(T)=\log\lambda\), then writes \(h(T)=\lambda\), and returns to metric isomorphism through the standard symbolic model.',r'For a hyperbolic two-torus automorphism, Haar entropy is \(\log\rho(A)\). More generally, in the mixing Anosov Markov construction, \(h_{\mathrm{top}}(T)=\log\lambda\), where \(\lambda\) is the Perron root and the stable-leaf scaling factor is \(\lambda^{-1}\). The equality \(h(T)=\lambda\) on the next notebook line omits the logarithm if the same symbol \(\lambda\) is intended. Equal Haar entropy classifies the two-torus automorphisms metrically as in 1967-138.2.',r'\citet[Theorem 5.3]{sinai1968markov} identifies topological entropy with the logarithm of the leaf/Perron factor. The metric classification itself is the already published Main Theorem of \citet{adlerweiss1967entropy}; its recurrence here remains expository.',('sinai1968markov','adlerweiss1967entropy'),cls='E',literal='false as written',match='partial',problem='GP-1967-03',reconstruction=r'The repeated theorem is separated from the erroneous second entropy line and from the stable-leaf scaling symbol.')
item(140,'P000228','construction','The two-rectangle torus partition and its connected refinement',r'Two parallelograms have sides along eigenlines through zero. The coarse itinerary can contain many points. Intersections \(V_i\cap TV_j\) are refined into connected components in four groups of sizes \(p,q,r,s\); one of the four displayed intersections is repeated.',r'For a real hyperbolic two-torus automorphism, a suitable two-rectangle partition is built from segments in its stable and unstable eigendirections. The component refinement of all four intersections \(V_i\cap TV_j\), \(i,j\in\{1,2\}\), gives a generating Markov partition with the four-block transition structure in 1967-138.9. The two coarse labels alone need not generate pointwise. All four ordered pairs are required; the duplicated off-diagonal line in the notes cannot be retained literally.',r'This is the geometric step in \citet[\S5]{adlerweiss1967entropy}, and it is expressly credited to Adler and Weiss in \citet[\S1, p.66]{sinai1968markov}. The source provides a proof-level exact match, not just thematic similarity to later symbolic dynamics.',('adlerweiss1967entropy','sinai1968markov'),cls='E',literal='false as written',problem='GP-1967-03',reconstruction=r'The four intersections are stated as the complete indexed family, without pretending the ambiguous overwritten second line identifies the author\'s intended ordering of \(q,r\).')
correction('P000224','Exponential separation on a compact manifold','Exponential growth of ambient distance for all forward or backward iterates','Use tangent-vector hyperbolicity, or local/intrinsic leaf distances within the valid range','Ambient distance is bounded by the diameter; Sinai1968 section1 uses induced leaf metrics for exponential estimates.')
correction('P000225','Existence of hyperbolic Lie-group automorphisms','Exist if and only if the Lie algebra is nilpotent','Nilpotence is not sufficient; a compatible hyperbolic lattice automorphism is required','The standard three-dimensional Heisenberg lattice forces a central derivative eigenvalue det B=plus or minus1.')
correction('P000228','Second entropy equation',r'h(T)=\lambda',r'h(T)=\log\lambda if lambda retains the preceding expanding-eigenvalue meaning','Both lines are visible in the scan; the earlier line and Sinai1968 Theorem5.3 identify the omitted logarithm.','both','ambiguity')
correction('P000228','Four rectangle intersections',r'V_1\cap TV_1; V_2\cap TV_2; V_1\cap TV_2; V_1\cap TV_2',r'The indexed family V_i\cap TV_j, i,j in {1,2}','The scan duplicates the final pair; Adler--Weiss1967 section5 uses all four pairs. The overwritten second index prevents a unique ordering-level transcription correction.','both','faithful_error')
save()

